## Program Constraints

- Production remains the public promotion target and is not an ordinary development environment.
- Development uses plain HTTP only on the LAN and Workpc localhost forward; production HTTPS
  controls remain intact.
- No production data, credentials, enrollments, sessions, writable mounts, or scheduler state is
  copied or redirected.
- The shared host and Docker daemon are accepted infrastructure boundaries; development container
  and web-service restarts must not restart production services.
- Exact implementation choices for the development root and Workpc tunnel service must preserve
  the transferred gates and remain recoverable.
- `work/tool-shed.yaml` changes to `split` only after M1 proves the development target and its
  operating contract.

## Verification Strategy

- Capture container, network, volume, bind-mount, database, environment, port, route, and
  production-health evidence before and after development deployment.
- Exercise direct LAN access from both test beds and localhost-only access from Workpc.
- Use manual enrollment/report handshakes before installing persistent development schedulers.
- Use deterministic synthetic data for migration, reset, and recovery tests.
- Bind promotion to the exact development-tested commit and recheck production health after it.

## Next Route

M1 and M2 are complete. M3 is the only remaining milestone and stays open at the protected
production boundary. When production promotion is intended, invoke an explicit Work5 route for
`IDEA-0018` or active release cohort `9902c070-34c6-4885-9f93-115fa6f59402`. Work5 must freeze the
complete candidate set, qualify migration and recovery, back up production before any
migration-bearing promotion, publish the exact frozen content, verify production and rollback, and
